<h1>Video 014 — main() method, part 2</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Language Fundamentals Part-14 || main() method part-2</p>
<p>YouTube has captions disabled on this upload. Continues the two-session main postmortem announced at the end of video 012. Command-line values (java Test A B C) are the next video, not this one.</p>
<h2>What this lecture covers</h2>
<ol><li>Overloading main</li></ol>
<ol><li>Which main the JVM picks</li></ol>
<ol><li>Inheritance / hiding of main in a child class</li></ol>
<ol><li>Static block vs main execution order</li></ol>
<ol><li>Class with static block but no main — Java 1.6 vs 1.7+ behaviour (Durga’s famous point)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Calling main yourself like a normal method</li></ol>
<h2>Detailed notes</h2>
<h3>Overloading `main`</h3>
<p>main is a method name. You can overload it like any other method.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;String[] main&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(int[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;int[] main&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(double d) {
        System.out.println(&quot;double main&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>`java Test` always calls `public static void main(String[] args)` (or the String... form). The other overloads are ordinary methods. They run only if you call them.</p>
<pre class='code'>public static void main(String[] args) {
    main(10.5);
    main(new int[]{1, 2, 3});
}</pre>
<p>Exam wording: “Can we overload main?” Yes. “Will JVM call the overloaded versions automatically?” No.</p>
<h3>Inheritance of `main`</h3>
<p>static methods are inherited in the “available on child” sense, but what happens is hiding, not instance overriding.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Parent {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Parent main&quot;);
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
}</pre>
<p>java Child</p>
<p>Output: `Parent main`</p>
<p>The child did not declare main, so JVM uses the inherited static main from Parent. Durga: you can run a child class that has no main written in its own file, if a parent provides the protocol method.</p>
<p>If the child declares its own:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Child extends Parent {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Child main&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>java Child   → Child main
java Parent  → Parent main</p>
<p>The child’s main hides the parent’s main. It is not override in the instance / @Override sense (static + main).</p>
<h3>Static block vs `main`</h3>
<p>Static blocks run when the class is initialized, which for java Test happens before main is invoked.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;static block&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;main method&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Output:</p>
<p>static block
main method</p>
<p>Multiple static blocks: top to bottom, then main.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static { System.out.println(&quot;block 1&quot;); }
    static { System.out.println(&quot;block 2&quot;); }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;main&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Output: block 1, block 2, main.</p>
<p>Static variable initializers are folded into the same class-init sequence (more in the OOPs static-control-flow videos later in the playlist).</p>
<h3>Class **without** `main` — version split Durga always teaches</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;static block&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Java 1.6 and earlier: JVM initializes the class, static block runs, then:</p>
<p>static block
NoSuchMethodError: main</p>
<p>Java 1.7 and later: JVM checks for main first. You get:</p>
<p>Error: Main method not found in class Test, please define the main method as:
   public static void main(String[] args)</p>
<p>and the static block does not run.</p>
<p>This is one of Durga’s highest-frequency interview points. Always state the version.</p>
<h3>Calling `main` yourself</h3>
<p>Nothing magical prevents:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;main&quot;);
        if (args.length == 0) {
            Test.main(new String[]{&quot;from code&quot;});
        }
    }
}</pre>
<p>That is a normal static call. Recursion without a guard → StackOverflowError.</p>
<p>JVM’s launch is only the first call. After that, main is just a method.</p>
<h3>`main` in an interface? in an enum?</h3>
<p>Later Java versions allow public static void main on an interface (Java 8+ static methods) or on an enum. This OCJP-era lecture is about classes. Don’t drag Java 8 interface main into this video unless you mark it as a later language change.</p>
<h3>Interaction with overloading + inheritance (exam combo)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;P&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public static void main(int[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;C int&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>java C → `P`</p>
<p>Child’s main(int[]) does not hide main(String[]). Different signature. JVM still finds P.main(String[]).</p>
<p>To hide, child must declare main(String[]) (or String...).</p>
<h2>Code from the lecture</h2>
<pre class='code'>class Parent {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Parent static&quot;);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Parent main&quot;);
        main(100);
    }
    public static void main(int x) {
        System.out.println(&quot;overloaded &quot; + x);
    }
}

class Child extends Parent {
    static {
        System.out.println(&quot;Child static&quot;);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Child main&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>java Child (typical): child static init (which may init parent first), then Child main. Parent’s main is hidden, so overloaded 100 does not run unless Child.main calls super — and super.main is not valid for static in that form; you would call Parent.main(args).</p>
<p>Parent init before child init is the static-control-flow rule (later videos). For this lecture, the takeaway is: the class you name on the command line is whose `String[] main` is searched, walking up the parent chain if missing.</p>
<h2>Rules to memorize</h2>
<ol><li>Overload main freely; JVM still only auto-calls String[] / String....</li></ol>
<ol><li>Child without main can still run if a parent has the protocol main.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Child main(String[]) hides parent main(String[]).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Static blocks of the launched class run before main (when the class is initialized).</li></ol>
<ol><li>No main at all: Java 6 runs static block then NoSuchMethodError; Java 7+ reports missing main and skips the static block.</li></ol>
<h2>Exam / interview traps</h2>
<ul><li>“We cannot overload main” — false.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Child with only main(int[]) — java Child still runs parent’s String[] main.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Static block without main — answer depends on Java version.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Main in child does not hide main in parent (case).</li></ul>
<h2>Recap</h2>
<p>main is an ordinary static method that the JVM happens to look for. Next lecture: the values passed into String[] args.</p>
<p>Next video: 015-command-line-arguments.md</p>
